Looking at the problem: `w1` acquires the permit twice, but the semaphore starts with only one permit. So `w1` acquires the first permit, then blocks forever waiting for a second permit that will never come (since `w1` itself holds the only permit and can't release it while blocked). This is a deadlock.

The requirement R4 says each worker may acquire more than once and must release exactly as many times as it acquired. R5 says while a worker waits, the holder must remain able to release. R6 says every schedule must terminate.

The fix: `w1` should acquire once, do its work, release, then acquire again, release again — i.e., acquire/release pairs rather than nested acquires. This way it never holds a permit while waiting for another.

```rust
use std::sync::{Arc, Condvar, Mutex};
use std::thread;

struct Semaphore {
    count: Mutex<usize>,
    cond: Condvar,
}

impl Semaphore {
    fn new(count: usize) -> Self {
        Semaphore {
            count: Mutex::new(count),
            cond: Condvar::new(),
        }
    }

    fn acquire(&self) {
        let mut count = self.count.lock().unwrap();
        while *count == 0 {
            count = self.cond.wait(count).unwrap();
        }
        *count -= 1;
    }

    fn release(&self) {
        let mut count = self.count.lock().unwrap();
        *count += 1;
        self.cond.notify_one();
    }
}

fn w1(s: Arc<Semaphore>) {
    s.acquire();
    s.release();
    s.acquire();
    s.release();
}

fn w2(s: Arc<Semaphore>) {
    s.acquire();
    s.release();
}

fn main() {
    let s = Arc::new(Semaphore::new(1));

    let s1 = Arc::clone(&s);
    let s2 = Arc::clone(&s);

    let h1 = thread::spawn(move || w1(s1));
    let h2 = thread::spawn(move || w2(s2));

    h1.join().unwrap();
    h2.join().unwrap();

    println!("DONE done=1");
}
```
